.md
## Safer Refresh and Animate
If you are refreshing something really fast, it can become a disaster if by accident you get an evaluated large expression. So not anymore, in such cases the refresh rate will be throtteled automatically down to 5 FPS or less depending on the size.

For example, it is known that `ContourPlot` generates an expression, which is hard for our built-in JIT to optimize for the real-time updates, therefore it falls back to the full reevaluation. Even if we set `RefreshRate` to a  high number it won't let it happen:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
## Safer Refresh and Animate
If you are refreshing something really fast, it can become a disaster if by accident you get an evaluated large expression. So not anymore, in such cases the refresh rate will be throtteled automatically down to 5 FPS or less depending on the size.

For example, it is known that `ContourPlot` generates an expression, which is hard for our built-in JIT to optimize for the real-time updates, therefore it falls back to the full reevaluation. Even if we set `RefreshRate` to a  high number it won't let it happen:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Animate[ContourPlot[(*FB[*)(((x - r))(*,*)/(*,*)((*SpB[*)Power[(x - r)(*|*),(*|*)2](*]SpB*) + (*SpB[*)Power[(y)(*|*),(*|*)2](*]SpB*)))(*]FB*), {x,-1,1}, {y,-1,1}], {r,-1,1,0.1}, (*BB[*)(RefreshRate->60)(*,*)(*"1:eJxTTMoPSmNkYGAoZgESHvk5KRAeB5AILqnMSXXKr0hjgskHleakFnMBGU6JydnpRfmleSlpzDDlQe5Ozvk5+UVFDGDwwR6dwcAAAAHdFiw="*)(*]BB*)]

