.md
## Improved notebook to HTML export
We removed all the clutter and workarounds persisted in our generated HTML notebooks


![](/attachments/Screenshot-2026-10-21-at-12.34.21%E2%80%AFPM-476.png)


It got smaller size, became search-engines friendly and is based on a [web-components](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Web_components). However, we retained the compatibillity with older format, so your old exported HTML notebooks can be reimported to `wln` format, and we not planning to remove the old parser


![](/attachments/Screenshot-2026-10-21-at-12.46.59%E2%80%AFPM-0ae.png)

*A single self-contained HTML file, that works without internet and can be reimported as a normal notebook*

We unified our approach in the way that whenever you export WLJS Notebook to MDX/React, vanilla HTML/JS or standalone HTML file:

1. All narrative cells (markdown) are rendered to a normal HTML, in a case of MDX it is left intact
2. All code cells's content is placed as it is and wrapped with `wljs-editor` tags

(2) is very important, since if this tag is not recognized by a browser, it will be shown as normal unstyled code. Once web-components boostrap is loaded, it automatically highlighted and becomes a normal-looking WLJS cell with graphics, sound, equations and etc. Here is our [bootstrap package](https://github.com/WLJSTeam/web-components), if you are React/Next.js user - no problem, [here is an example](https://github.com/WLJSTeam/web-components-mdx) for you. This is how it looks like in practice if you export the notebook as <small>MDX</small> or <small>HTML embeddable</small>


%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
## Improved notebook to HTML export
We removed all the clutter and workarounds persisted in our generated HTML notebooks


![](/attachments/Screenshot-2026-10-21-at-12.34.21%E2%80%AFPM-476.png)


It got smaller size, became search-engines friendly and is based on a [web-components](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Web_components). However, we retained the compatibillity with older format, so your old exported HTML notebooks can be reimported to `wln` format, and we not planning to remove the old parser


![](/attachments/Screenshot-2026-10-21-at-12.46.59%E2%80%AFPM-0ae.png)

*A single self-contained HTML file, that works without internet and can be reimported as a normal notebook*

We unified our approach in the way that whenever you export WLJS Notebook to MDX/React, vanilla HTML/JS or standalone HTML file:

1. All narrative cells (markdown) are rendered to a normal HTML, in a case of MDX it is left intact
2. All code cells's content is placed as it is and wrapped with `wljs-editor` tags

(2) is very important, since if this tag is not recognized by a browser, it will be shown as normal unstyled code. Once web-components boostrap is loaded, it automatically highlighted and becomes a normal-looking WLJS cell with graphics, sound, equations and etc. Here is our [bootstrap package](https://github.com/WLJSTeam/web-components), if you are React/Next.js user - no problem, [here is an example](https://github.com/WLJSTeam/web-components-mdx) for you. This is how it looks like in practice if you export the notebook as <small >MDX</small> or <small >HTML embeddable</small>

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.html
<h2>Contour Plot</h2>
<p>Generates a contour plot of f as a function of {x,y}</p>


<wljs-editor display="codemirror" type="Input">
  <xmp>ContourPlot[
  Cos[x] + Cos[y], {x, 0, 4 Pi}, {y, 0, 4 Pi}
]</xmp>
</wljs-editor>

<wljs-editor display="codemirror" type="Output">
  <xmp>(*VB[*)(FrontEndRef["..."])(*,*)(*"1:..."*)(*]VB*)</xmp>
</wljs-editor>

